      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious conservatives generally argue that lawful physician-assisted dying violates the sanctity of life and the inherent dignity of individuals, viewing human life as sacred from conception to natural death. In contrast, religious liberals tend to emphasize autonomy and compassion, arguing that protecting vulnerable individuals from unbearable suffering requires supporting their right to choose death. While conservative ethics prioritize the inviolability of life itself, liberal perspectives often balance bodily integrity against the moral imperative to prevent agony. Ultimately, both sides navigate the tension between tradition and contemporary understandings of human suffering and dignity.",
